  "live_records": {
    "q-10206": {
      "dom_id": "step-q10206",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2},\\,y=t+{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,1].$",
      "worked_solution": "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+{t}^{3})(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1}+4\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+4(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
