    "q-15694": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15694",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253507/assets/e24e093814eeaaf82b1c7e2d5c60648407f9f5aaf717de1138f08505b579651e.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{-t},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$",
      "worked_solution": "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(-2{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t)=6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-15694-a",
