    "q-15699": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15699",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=({t}^{2}-1){e}^{t},\\,y={t}^{3}+1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [1,2]?$",
      "worked_solution": "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}-1){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2t{e}^{t}+({t}^{2}-1){e}^{t} \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+2t-1){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{3}+1)({t}^{2}+2t-1){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [1,2].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{3}+1)({t}^{2}+2t-1){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
